DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       For an overview of the fanotify API, see ffaannoottiiffyy(7).

       ffaannoottiiffyy__mmaarrkk()  adds,  removes,  or  modifies  an  fanotify  mark on a
       filesystem object.   The  caller  must  have  read  permission  on  the
       filesystem object that is to be marked.

       The  _f_a_n_o_t_i_f_y___f_d  argument  is  a  file  descriptor  returned  by  ffaann‐‐
       oottiiffyy__iinniitt(2).

       _f_l_a_g_s is a bit mask describing the modification to  perform.   It  must
       include exactly one of the following values:

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__AADDDD
              The events in _m_a_s_k will be added to the mark mask (or to the ig‐
              nore  mask).  _m_a_s_k must be nonempty or the error EEIINNVVAALL will oc‐
              cur.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__RREEMMOOVVEE
              The events in argument _m_a_s_k will be removed from the  mark  mask
              (or  from  the ignore mask).  _m_a_s_k must be nonempty or the error
              EEIINNVVAALL will occur.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__FFLLUUSSHH
              Remove either all marks for filesystems, all marks  for  mounts,
              or  all marks for directories and files from the fanotify group.
              If _f_l_a_g_s contains FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMOOUUNNTT, all marks for mounts  are  re‐
              moved  from  the  group.  If _f_l_a_g_s contains FFAANN__MMAARRKK__FFIILLEESSYYSSTTEEMM,
              all marks for filesystems are removed from  the  group.   Other‐
              wise,  all marks for directories and files are removed.  No flag
              other than, and  at  most  one  of,  the  flags  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMNNTTNNSS,
              FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMOOUUNNTT,  or  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__FFIILLEESSYYSSTTEEMM can be used in conjunc‐
              tion with FFAANN__MMAARRKK__FFLLUUSSHH.  _m_a_s_k is ignored.

       If none of the values above is specified, or more than  one  is  speci‐
       fied, the call fails with the error EEIINNVVAALL.

       In  addition,  zero  or  more  of the following values may be ORed into
       _f_l_a_g_s:

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__DDOONNTT__FFOOLLLLOOWW
              If _p_a_t_h is a symbolic link, mark the link  itself,  rather  than
              the  file  to  which  it  refers.   (By default, ffaannoottiiffyy__mmaarrkk()
              dereferences _p_a_t_h if it is a symbolic link.)

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__OONNLLYYDDIIRR
              If the filesystem object to be marked is not  a  directory,  the
              error EENNOOTTDDIIRR shall be raised.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMNNTTNNSS (since Linux 6.14)
              Mark  the  mount  namespace specified by _p_a_t_h.  If _p_a_t_h does not
              represent a mount namespace (e.g.  _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_n_s_/_m_n_t),  the  call
              fails  with  the  error EEIINNVVAALL.  An fanotify group that was ini‐
              tialized with flag FFAANN__RREEPPOORRTT__MMNNTT is required.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMOOUUNNTT
              Mark the mount specified by _p_a_t_h.  If _p_a_t_h is not itself a mount
              point, the mount containing _p_a_t_h will be marked.   All  directo‐
              ries,  subdirectories, and the contained files of the mount will
              be monitored.  The events which require that filesystem  objects
              are  identified by file handles, such as FFAANN__CCRREEAATTEE, FFAANN__AATTTTRRIIBB,
              FFAANN__MMOOVVEE, and FFAANN__DDEELLEETTEE__SSEELLFF, cannot be provided as a _m_a_s_k when
              _f_l_a_g_s contains FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMOOUUNNTT.  Attempting to do so will  result
              in  the  error EEIINNVVAALL being returned.  Use of this flag requires
              the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__FFIILLEESSYYSSTTEEMM (since Linux 4.20)
              Mark the filesystem specified by _p_a_t_h.  The filesystem  contain‐
              ing  _p_a_t_h  will be marked.  All the contained files and directo‐
              ries of the filesystem from any mount point will  be  monitored.
              Use of this flag requires the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__MMAASSKK
              The  events in _m_a_s_k shall be added to or removed from the ignore
              mask.  Note that the  flags  FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR,  and  FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__OONN__CCHHIILLDD
              have no effect when provided with this flag.  The effect of set‐
              ting  the  flags  FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR,  and FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__OONN__CCHHIILLDD in the mark
              mask on the events that are set in the ignore mask is  undefined
              and depends on the Linux kernel version.  Specifically, prior to
              Linux  5.9, setting a mark mask on a file and a mark with ignore
              mask on its parent directory would not result in ignoring events
              on the file, regardless of the FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__OONN__CCHHIILLDD  flag  in  the
              parent  directory’s  mark mask.  When the ignore mask is updated
              with the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__MMAASSKK flag on a mark  that  was  previ‐
              ously  updated  with  the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE flag, the update fails
              with EEEEXXIISSTT error.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE (since Linux 6.0, 5.15.154, and 5.10.220)
              This flag has a  similar  effect  as  setting  the  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGG‐‐
              NNOORREEDD__MMAASSKK  flag.   The  events in _m_a_s_k shall be added to or re‐
              moved from the ignore mask.   Unlike  the  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__MMAASSKK
              flag,  this  flag  also  has  the effect that the FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR, and
              FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__OONN__CCHHIILLDD  flags  take  effect  on  the  ignore   mask.
              Specifically, unless the FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR flag is set with FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGG‐‐
              NNOORREE,  events  on  directories will not be ignored.  If the flag
              FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__OONN__CCHHIILLDD is set with FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE, events on  chil‐
              dren  will  be ignored.  For example, a mark on a directory with
              combination of a mask with FFAANN__CCRREEAATTEE event and  FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR  flag
              and  an  ignore mask with FFAANN__CCRREEAATTEE event and without FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR
              flag, will result in getting only the  events  for  creation  of
              sub‐directories.   When using the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE flag to add to
              an ignore mask of a mount, filesystem, or directory inode  mark,
              the  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__SSUURRVV__MMOODDIIFFYY flag must be specified.  Fail‐
              ure to do so will results with EEIINNVVAALL or EEIISSDDIIRR error.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__SSUURRVV__MMOODDIIFFYY
              The ignore mask shall survive modify events.  If  this  flag  is
              not  set,  the ignore mask is cleared when a modify event occurs
              on the marked object.  Omitting this flag is typically  used  to
              suppress events (e.g., FFAANN__OOPPEENN) for a specific file, until that
              specific  file’s content has been modified.  It is far less use‐
              ful to suppress events on an entire filesystem, or mount, or  on
              all files inside a directory, until some file’s content has been
              modified.   For  this  reason, the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE flag requires
              the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__SSUURRVV__MMOODDIIFFYY flag on a mount, filesystem, or
              directory inode mark.  This flag cannot be removed from  a  mark
              once  set.  When the ignore mask is updated without this flag on
              a mark that was previously updated with the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE  and
              FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREEDD__SSUURRVV__MMOODDIIFFYY flags, the update fails with EEEEXXIISSTT
              error.

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE__SSUURRVV
              This    is    a    synonym   for   (FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGGNNOORREE|FFAANN__MMAARRKK__IIGG‐‐
              NNOORREEDD__SSUURRVV__MMOODDIIFFYY).

       FFAANN__MMAARRKK__EEVVIICCTTAABBLLEE (since Linux 5.19, 5.15.154, and 5.10.220)
              When an inode mark is created with this flag, the  inode  object
              will  not  be pinned to the inode cache, therefore, allowing the
              inode object to be evicted from the inode cache when the  memory
              pressure  on  the system is high.  The eviction of the inode ob‐
              ject results in the evictable mark also being  lost.   When  the
              mask  of  an  evictable  inode mark is updated without using the
              FFAANN__MMAARRKK__EEVVIICCAATTBBLLEE flag, the marked inode  is  pinned  to  inode
              cache  and  the mark is no longer evictable.  When the mask of a
              non‐evictable inode mark is updated with the  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__EEVVIICCTTAABBLLEE
              flag,  the inode mark remains non‐evictable and the update fails
              with EEEEXXIISSTT error.  Mounts and filesystems are not evictable ob‐
              jects, therefore, an  attempt  to  create  a  mount  mark  or  a
              filesystem mark with the FFAANN__MMAARRKK__EEVVIICCTTAABBLLEE flag, will result in
              the  error EEIINNVVAALL.  For example, inode marks can be used in com‐
              bination with mount marks to reduce the amount  of  events  from
              noninteresting  paths.   The event listener reads events, checks
              if the path reported in the event is of interest, and if  it  is
              not,  the listener sets a mark with an ignore mask on the direc‐
              tory.  Evictable inode marks allow using this method for a large
              number of directories without the concern of pinning all  inodes
              and exhausting the system’s memory.

       _m_a_s_k  defines which events shall be listened for (or which shall be ig‐
       nored).  It is a bit mask composed of the following values:

       FFAANN__AACCCCEESSSS
              Create an event when a file or directory (but see BUGS)  is  ac‐
              cessed (read).

       FFAANN__MMOODDIIFFYY
              Create an event when a file is modified (write).

       FFAANN__CCLLOOSSEE__WWRRIITTEE
              Create an event when a writable file is closed.

       FFAANN__CCLLOOSSEE__NNOOWWRRIITTEE
              Create an event when a read‐only file or directory is closed.

       FFAANN__OOPPEENN
              Create an event when a file or directory is opened.

       FFAANN__OOPPEENN__EEXXEECC (since Linux 5.0)
              Create an event when a file is opened with the intent to be exe‐
              cuted.  See NOTES for additional details.

       FFAANN__AATTTTRRIIBB (since Linux 5.1)
              Create  an  event  when the metadata for a file or directory has
              changed.  An fanotify group that identifies  filesystem  objects
              by file handles is required.

       FFAANN__CCRREEAATTEE (since Linux 5.1)
              Create  an  event when a file or directory has been created in a
              marked parent directory.   An  fanotify  group  that  identifies
              filesystem objects by file handles is required.

       FFAANN__DDEELLEETTEE (since Linux 5.1)
              Create  an  event when a file or directory has been deleted in a
              marked parent directory.   An  fanotify  group  that  identifies
              filesystem objects by file handles is required.

       FFAANN__DDEELLEETTEE__SSEELLFF (since Linux 5.1)
              Create  an  event  when  a  marked  file  or directory itself is
              deleted.  An fanotify group that identifies  filesystem  objects
              by file handles is required.

       FFAANN__MMOOVVEEDD__FFRROOMM (since Linux 5.1)
              Create  an  event when a file or directory has been moved from a
              marked parent directory.   An  fanotify  group  that  identifies
              filesystem objects by file handles is required.

       FFAANN__MMOOVVEEDD__TTOO (since Linux 5.1)
              Create  an  event  when  a file or directory has been moved to a
              marked parent directory.   An  fanotify  group  that  identifies
              filesystem objects by file handles is required.

       FFAANN__RREENNAAMMEE (since Linux 5.17, 5.15.154, and 5.10.220)
              This  event  contains  the  same  information provided by events
              FFAANN__MMOOVVEEDD__FFRROOMM and FFAANN__MMOOVVEEDD__TTOO, however  is  represented  by  a
              single  event  with  up to two information records.  An fanotify
              group that identifies filesystem objects by file handles is  re‐
              quired.   If  the filesystem object to be marked is not a direc‐
              tory, the error EENNOOTTDDIIRR shall be raised.

       FFAANN__MMOOVVEE__SSEELLFF (since Linux 5.1)
              Create an event when a marked file or directory itself has  been
              moved.   An fanotify group that identifies filesystem objects by
              file handles is required.

       FFAANN__MMNNTT__AATTTTAACCHH
       FFAANN__MMNNTT__DDEETTAACCHH (both since Linux 6.14)
              Create an event when a mount was attached to or detached from  a
              marked  mount  namespace,  respectively.  An attempt to set this
              flag on an inode, mount, or filesystem mark will result  in  the
              error  EEIINNVVAALL.  An fanotify group that was initialized with flag
              FFAANN__RREEPPOORRTT__MMNNTT and the mark flag  FFAANN__MMAARRKK__MMNNTTNNSS  are  required.
              An additional information record of type FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__IINNFFOO__TTYYPPEE__MMNNTT
              is  returned with the event.  See ffaannoottiiffyy(7) for additional de‐
              tails.

       FFAANN__FFSS__EERRRROORR (since Linux 5.16, 5.15.154, and 5.10.220)
              Create an event when a filesystem error leading to  inconsistent
              filesystem  metadata  is  detected.   An  additional information
              record of type FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__IINNFFOO__TTYYPPEE__EERRRROORR is  returned  for  each
              event  in  the  read  buffer.  An fanotify group that identifies
              filesystem objects by file handles is required.  Events of  such
              type  are  dependent  on support from the underlying filesystem.
              At the  time  of  writing,  only  the  eexxtt44  filesystem  reports
              FFAANN__FFSS__EERRRROORR events.  See ffaannoottiiffyy(7) for additional details.

       FFAANN__OOPPEENN__PPEERRMM
              Create an event when a permission to open a file or directory is
              requested.    An   fanotify   file   descriptor   created   with
              FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__PPRREE__CCOONNTTEENNTT or FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__CCOONNTTEENNTT is required.

       FFAANN__OOPPEENN__EEXXEECC__PPEERRMM (since Linux 5.0)
              Create an event when a permission to open a file  for  execution
              is   requested.    An  fanotify  file  descriptor  created  with
              FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__PPRREE__CCOONNTTEENNTT or  FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__CCOONNTTEENNTT  is  required.   See
              NOTES for additional details.

       FFAANN__AACCCCEESSSS__PPEERRMM
              Create an event when a permission to read a file or directory is
              requested.    An   fanotify   file   descriptor   created   with
              FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__PPRREE__CCOONNTTEENNTT or FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__CCOONNTTEENNTT is required.

       FFAANN__PPRREE__AACCCCEESSSS (since Linux 6.14)
              Create an event before read or write access  to  a  file  range,
              that  provides  an  opportunity for the event listener to modify
              the content of the file before access  to  the  content  in  the
              specified  range.   An  additional  information  record  of type
              FFAANN__EEVVEENNTT__IINNFFOO__TTYYPPEE__RRAANNGGEE is returned for each event in the read
              buffer.    An   fanotify   file    descriptor    created    with
              FFAANN__CCLLAASSSS__PPRREE__CCOONNTTEENNTT is required.

       FFAANN__OONNDDIIRR
              Create  events  for  directories—for  example,  when ooppeennddiirr(3),
              rreeaaddddiirr(3) (but see BUGS), and cclloosseeddiirr(3) are called.   Without
